12.. Mayor Semeta to present the Mayor’s HB Excellence Award to Greg Schooley, Information Technology Technician I  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Semeta introduced Greg Schooley, Information Technology Technician, who was nominated by fellow employees for the Mayor's HB Excellence Award.  Mr. Schooley was described as someone who can quickly determine a solution for technology challenges while displaying an attitude of public service as he puts the needs of his customers before his own.  He is a humble, smart, and ambitious individual who was instrumental in helping many set up remote operations due to COVID-19.  Mr. Schooley routinely goes above and beyond in being adaptable, and displays positive "can do" energy for every assignment.\par
}


10.. Mayor Semeta to call on Victoria Alberty to present the “Adoptable Pet of the Month”  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Victoria Alberty, Waggin' Trails Rescue Foundation, introduced Bailey, an 8-year old Maltese/Yorkie mix, currently being fostered at Top Dog Barkery.  Bailey was described as hypoallergenic and super energetic, who was raised in a loving home where family circumstances forced him into adoption.  Additional details can be found at waggintrailsrescue.com.\par
}


13.. Recognition of Retiring Police Chief Rob Handy  -  Action: agenda title only {\rtf1\ansi\ansicpg1252\deff0\deflang1033{\fonttbl{\f0\fnil\fcharset0 Arial;}}
{\colortbl ;\red0\green0\blue0;}
\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\fs24 Mayor Semeta introduced Police Chief Handy and described highlights from his seven years of service as he focused on balancing the needs of the business community while maintaining the highest quality of life standards for the residents in acknowledging his excellent performance.\par
\par
A video presentation for Chief Handy with various members of the Huntington Beach Police and Community Foundation sharing their appreciation for his excellent service and leadership.\par
\par
John Etheridge, President, Huntington Beach Police and Community Foundation, thanked Retired Chief Handy for his service and presented him with a commemoration plaque. \par
\par
Elsa Greenfield, Director, HB Youth Shelter and Waymakers Gang Prevention and Youth Development Program, expressed appreciation to Retired Chief Handy for implementing the Spanish Citizens' Academy which developed trust within the Oak View community, and thanked him for his tenacity, selflessness, dedication and commitment to the community.\par
\par
Tonya Hoxsie, Chief Executive Officer, Boys &amp; Girls Clubs of Huntington Valley, called in to share her appreciation for the many ways that Rob Handy has supported her and the Clubs during his tenure as Chief of Police.\par
\par
Kelly Miller, President &amp; CEO, Visit Huntington Beach, shared that Chief Handy redefined what policing is all about, and his empathy and interest in understanding others were crucial to his successful service and leadership, especially during this past summer, and thanked Chief Handy for helping people to feel safer in Huntington Beach.\par
\par
Tim Whitacre, representing Orange County Supervisor Michelle Steel, presented a commendation to Chief Handy in recognition of his exceptional leadership and example for others to follow.\par
\par
Alexander Gonzalez, Regional Director, Office of Congressman Harley Rouda, presented a certificate of Special Congressional Recognition in appreciation for Chief Handy's dedicated service and loyal professionalism to the City of Huntington.\par
\par
Brett Barnes, Owner, Duke's Huntington Beach, expressed appreciation for Chief Handy's listening skills, solutions and leadership for the Downtown business community in making Huntington Beach a safe destination for visitors, and his open accessibility for both big and small issues.\par
\par
City Manager Oliver Chi reviewed especially this past year of mostly COVID-19 related situations and expressed appreciative recognition for Chief Handy's adaptability and solutions-based approach to the many challenges, and presented a "Handy Harbour, 2013 -- 2020" street sign as a gift from staff.\par
\par
Mayor Semeta presented a commendation to Chief Handy on behalf of the City Council to acknowledge his seven years of outstanding service and leadership for all of Huntington Beach.\par
\par
Chief Handy thanked City Council and staff for their support over his years of service; expressed appreciation to the Executive Team for being the best to work with and all sharing the common purpose of serving businesses, residents and visitors; thanked his family, including parents, in-laws, wife Jennifer and daughters Matelyn and Kaitlyn; thanked the community for support, partnerships and commitment to solve problems and prevent crime; commended the Command staff for successfully meeting the challenges created by COVID-19 this year, as well as the Leadership Team, Line Level Officers and Support Staff who were all impacted by new routines and realities and still served with daily professionalism, discipline, and dedication to duty and service.  Chief Handy stated that his last seven years of service in Huntington Beach were the most honorable of his 30 year law enforcement career.  He thanked those who stepped up to make the Community Foundation a reality and highlighted some of the ways their fundraising of almost $1M in five years has benefited not only the community but also Police Department staff.\par
}


